{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 argues that decentralized networks are less resilient because operators react with similar 'panic' heuristics during systemic stress, creating a correlated human-error failure mode. This mechanism presumes that monolithic operators behave fundamentally differently—yet it provides no causal argument for why the centralized operators of a single large service would be immune to the same panic heuristics or would not also issue simultaneous restarts or identical config changes under systemic stress. Worse, in a monolith there is a single operational authority, so a single bad decision propagates by construction; the causal mechanism actually predicts *higher* human-error correlation in the monolith, not lower. SRC_001 thus fails to establish the key comparative causal link its claim depends on."},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's mechanism—market pressures forcing shared managed services—does not distinguish the failure modes it needs to. It asserts the monolith's redundancy is 'explicitly engineered and monitored' and therefore more robust, but if the decentralized operators are all on AWS EKS, that homogeneity is itself evidence of a *shared cause* that would eventually produce correlated failure (e.g., an AWS regional event). SRC_002 does not explain why an explicitly engineered monolith built on that same substrate is immune to the very shared cause it identifies. It thus conflates 'redundancy is engineered' with 'redundancy survives correlated shared-cause failure' without supplying the causal mechanism for that survival."}]}